## Exploration Before Action

- **You must explore before you edit.** Before touching any file, you must have read (1) the file itself, (2) at least one caller or consumer of the code you're changing, and (3) the relevant test file if one exists. If you cannot name the callers, you haven't explored enough.
- **For unfamiliar directories, call `select_context_tool` first.** If you haven't worked in a directory this session, cognitive-cache tells you what matters before you start guessing. This is not optional for new territory.
- **Read the test before writing the fix.** Tests encode the contract. If you change behavior without reading the test, you're guessing what the contract is. Read it, understand what's asserted, then decide if the contract or the implementation is wrong.
- **Trace the call chain.** Before modifying a function, identify who calls it and what they expect. A grep for the function name is the minimum. If it's exported or public, understand the interface contract before changing it.
- **State what you found before proposing changes.** When reporting to the user, lead with evidence (file paths, line numbers, actual behavior observed), then your interpretation, then your proposed action. Never skip straight to "here's the fix."
